<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Allat</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as al-Lat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Allat was a pre-Islamic Arabian goddess worshipped widely across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and the Syrian desert, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taif">Ta'if</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a> and Hatra. In Palmyra she was identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> she was one of the three goddesses named in the Qur'an together with al-Uzza and Manat. Her shrine at Ta'if was destroyed after the coming of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/allat.jpg" alt="Allat" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Allat_Palmyra_RGZM_3369.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
